Neuro-Aerokinematic Augmentation: A Unified Rate-Distortion Framework for Brain‑Controlled Supernumerary Locomotor and Flight Exoskeletons

Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Gemini, Deepseek 

Abstract

We prove that the maximum running speed and sustained lift a human can achieve by simultaneously controlling supernumerary legs and wings via a non‑invasive brain–computer interface (BCI) are limited by a single informational invariant — the neural channel capacity — and not by actuator power, material strength, or aerodynamic surface area. The result is formalised as a vector rate‑distortion theorem that links the mutual information of motor‑imagery EEG to the minimum achievable covariance of the full 28‑dimensional augmented‑body kinematic tracking error. We derive exact critical capacities below which either gait bifurcation (loss of stable running) or dynamic stall (loss of lift) becomes inevitable in finite time with probability one, regardless of the actuation technology. For Earth‑ambient conditions the critical information transfer rate (ITR) is ≈ 385 bits/min for stable running and ≈ 410 bits/min for sustained flapping flight; current non‑invasive BCIs deliver 20–60 bits/min.

From this informational ceiling we construct a shared‑autonomy architecture: the human controls a 4‑dimensional subspace of the combined leg‑wing velocity manifold — the directions of maximal speed/lift authority per unit of balance and vestibular disturbance — while a model‑predictive‑control copilot enforces safety and efficiency at 100 Hz. Safety is guaranteed by a unified zero‑moment‑point and vestibular control barrier function that renders the safe set forward‑invariant under bounded harness compliance and neuromuscular delays. Stability of the coupled human‑machine system under the total 150 ms neuromechanical lag and 100 ms aerodynamic stall delay is proven via a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional that explicitly accounts for the state‑dependent delay structure, yielding input‑to‑state stability with respect to decoder error and wind disturbances.

The augmented body schema is described as a Riemannian product manifold equipped with the kinetic‑energy metric; the decoder’s training objective is to preserve orthogonality between biological‑limb and supernumerary‑limb tangent spaces, formalising the neurological independence required for embodiment. A falsifiable minimum‑viable experiment — a VR‑based treadmill‑and‑flight simulator — is specified, and we provide the complete mathematical derivation of every threshold, Jacobian, and stability condition from first principles. The framework establishes that unless a breakthrough in neural interface bandwidth occurs, the machine must become the spinal cord and cerebellum of the augmented human, handling all reflexive coordination while the brain supplies only velocity and heading goals.

