Chapter 1 — Introduction

1.1 The Body Without Organs

Every tool‑using species eventually asks: where does the body end? The answer has migrated outward — from skin to hand, from hand to tool, from tool to prosthetic, from prosthetic to neurally integrated machine. A brain–computer interface (BCI) that decodes motor imagery and provides congruent sensory feedback begins, neurologically, to be incorporated into the body schema. The last two decades have demonstrated that humans can learn to control a third robotic arm (supernumerary limb) while using their biological limbs (Lisini Baldi et al., 2017), and that powered lower‑limb exoskeletons can amplify locomotor force beyond biological limits (Mooney et al., 2014). In parallel, flapping‑wing micro‑air vehicles have proven that unsteady aerodynamics can generate lift at human scales (Keennon et al., 2012). The present thesis asks whether these three technologies — supernumerary legs, powered wings, and non‑invasive BCI — can be unified under a principled, provably‑safe theoretical framework to create a human capable of both supersonic running and sustained, self‑powered flight.

The central engineering obstacle is neither structural nor propulsive. Contemporary series‑elastic actuators can deliver ground reaction forces sufficient for 15–20 m/s running; carbon‑fibre springs can store and return energy at efficiencies exceeding biological tendons; and flapping wings with 4 m² area can, in principle, generate enough unsteady lift to support a 70 kg human. The true bottleneck is informational. The non‑invasive EEG channel that conveys motor imagery from brain to machine has a finite information‑theoretic capacity. The kinematic complexity of coordinating four supernumerary legs and two wings — a state space of 28 dimensions — demands a control bandwidth that far exceeds what current neural interfaces can supply.

This thesis formulates and proves the Informational Ceiling Hypothesis:

(H1) The maximum speed v_{\max} and maximum sustained lift L_{\max} achievable by a BCI‑controlled leg‑wing exoskeleton are bounded by the information transfer rate (ITR, bits per minute) of the neural interface. For any actuation technology, there exist critical thresholds \mathrm{ITR}_{\mathrm{crit}}^{\mathrm{run}}, \mathrm{ITR}_{\mathrm{crit}}^{\mathrm{fly}} such that if \mathrm{ITR} < \mathrm{ITR}_{\mathrm{crit}}, the probability of maintaining a stable running orbit or a stable flight envelope decays to zero in finite time, regardless of actuator power or aerodynamic surface area.

The null hypothesis (H0) — that current BCIs already supply sufficient ITR — is explicitly falsified by the quantitative bounds derived in Chapter 3.

1.2 Unified Testable Predictions

1. Saturation of performance with ITR. For the combined leg‑wing system, plotting v_{\max} or L_{\max} against \mathrm{ITR} will show a sharp threshold behaviour: negligible performance below \mathrm{ITR}_{\mathrm{crit}}, a rapid rise near the critical value, and saturation at the actuation‑limited ceiling. The exponential form sometimes hypothesized is an artefact of scalar approximations; the true curve is a step function smoothed only by inter‑stride/inter‑stroke variance.
2. Effective user‑controlled dimensionality. For a given ITR, independently controlling more than
   p_{\mathrm{eff}} = \left\lfloor \frac{ \mathrm{ITR} }{ f_s \log_2(1 + \mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{min}}) } \right\rfloor
   \]  
   parameters does not improve performance and rapidly degrades safety. \mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{min}} is the minimum signal‑to‑noise ratio required per channel to keep the tracking error covariance below the safety margin.
3. Mandatory shared autonomy. Whenever \mathrm{ITR} < \mathrm{ITR}_{\mathrm{crit}}, a shared‑autonomy architecture (AI copilot handling joint‑level coordination) will always outperform direct human control in terms of both speed/lift and safety metrics.

1.3 Scope and Structure

The dissertation develops the complete mathematical infrastructure for the hypothesis. Chapter 2 presents all necessary mathematical preliminaries, rigorously derived from first principles, including Riemannian geometry of kinetic‑energy metrics, input‑to‑state stability for delay systems, control barrier functions, and rate‑distortion theory for vector Gaussian sources. Chapter 3 defines the unified augmented‑body model, derives the combined rate‑distortion bound, computes the critical ITR for Earth conditions, and proves the inevitability of loss‑of‑stability below that threshold. The remaining chapters — only outlined here — will specify the shared‑autonomy copilot, the Lyapunov–Krasovskii stability proof for the full delayed system, the optimal user subspace via GSVD, the afferent feedback design, and the experimental validation protocol. The appendices provide complete derivations of the SLIP Poincaré map, the unsteady blade‑element model, and the CBF construction.

The work does not address untethered power supply, material fatigue, or the aerodynamics of supersonic running (which would require compressibility corrections). It restricts itself to Earth atmospheric conditions; off‑world scaling is a trivial extension of the same rate‑distortion logic, not a separate physics problem.

