Chapter 2 — Mathematical Preliminaries

This chapter provides a self‑contained exposition of the mathematical tools required. Every definition is stated and every theorem used in later chapters is proved or cited with an explicit reference.

2.1 Riemannian Geometry of Mechanical Systems

Definition 2.1 (Riemannian Manifold). A smooth manifold \mathcal{M} equipped with a smoothly varying inner product g_p : T_p\mathcal{M} \times T_p\mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R} on each tangent space is a Riemannian manifold. The metric g defines lengths of curves and distances.

Definition 2.2 (Kinetic‑Energy Metric). For a mechanical system with configuration manifold \mathcal{Q} and inertia tensor \mathbf{M}(\mathbf{q}), the kinetic energy is T = \frac{1}{2}\dot{\mathbf{q}}^\top \mathbf{M}(\mathbf{q})\dot{\mathbf{q}}. This induces a Riemannian metric on \mathcal{Q} via g_{\mathbf{q}}(\mathbf{v},\mathbf{w}) = \mathbf{v}^\top \mathbf{M}(\mathbf{q})\mathbf{w}.

Product Structure. The augmented body configuration manifold is \mathcal{Q} = \mathcal{Q}_h \times \mathcal{Q}_\ell \times \mathcal{Q}_w, where \mathcal{Q}_h is the human biological joint space (20 DOF), \mathcal{Q}_\ell the supernumerary leg space (8 DOF), and \mathcal{Q}_w the wing space (8 DOF). Because the inertia tensor is block‑diagonal with respect to these subsystems when the harness is rigid, the kinetic‑energy metric decomposes as

g = g_h \oplus g_\ell \oplus g_w.
\]  

The tangent bundle splits orthogonally: T\mathcal{Q} = T\mathcal{Q}_h \oplus T\mathcal{Q}_\ell \oplus T\mathcal{Q}_w. Any vector field \mathbf{V} on \mathcal{Q} that is tangent to one factor has zero Lie bracket with any vector field tangent to another factor: [\mathbf{V}_h, \mathbf{V}_\ell] = 0, etc. This trivial geometric fact means that separate control of the subsystems is possible provided the controller does not introduce cross‑couplings. The BCI decoder’s task is precisely to learn a map \Phi: \mathcal{M}_h \to \mathcal{Q} such that the pushforward of a biological motor command has zero component in T\mathcal{Q}_\ell \oplus T\mathcal{Q}_w. This is a learning problem, not a geometric one; the Riemannian submersion language used in earlier frameworks is mathematically accurate but adds no physical insight. We therefore omit further differential‑geometric elaboration.

2.2 Stability of Delay Systems: Lyapunov–Krasovskii Functionals and ISS

Consider a retarded functional differential equation

\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = \mathbf{f}(t, \mathbf{x}_t, \mathbf{u}(t)), \quad \mathbf{x}_t(\theta) = \mathbf{x}(t+\theta),\; \theta \in [-\tau, 0],
\]  

with initial condition \mathbf{x}_0 = \boldsymbol{\phi} \in C([-\tau,0],\mathbb{R}^n). The state lives in the infinite‑dimensional space of continuous functions.

Definition 2.3 (ISS for Time‑Delay Systems). The system is input‑to‑state stable (ISS) if there exist \beta \in \mathcal{KL} and \gamma \in \mathcal{K} such that for any bounded input \mathbf{u}(\cdot),

\|\mathbf{x}(t)\| \le \beta(\|\boldsymbol{\phi}\|_{\infty}, t) + \gamma\!\left(\sup_{0 \le s \le t} \|\mathbf{u}(s)\|\right), \quad \forall t \ge 0.

Theorem 2.1 (Lyapunov–Krasovskii ISS). Suppose there exists a continuous functional V : C([-\tau,0],\mathbb{R}^n) \to \mathbb{R}^+ and constants \alpha_1, \alpha_2, \alpha_3 > 0 such that

\alpha_1\|\boldsymbol{\phi}(0)\|^2 \le V(\boldsymbol{\phi}) \le \alpha_2\|\boldsymbol{\phi}\|_{\infty}^2,
\]  

and along trajectories,

\dot{V}(\mathbf{x}_t) \le -\alpha_3 \|\mathbf{x}(t)\|^2 + \sigma(\|\mathbf{u}(t)\|),
\]  

with \sigma \in \mathcal{K}. Then the system is ISS with respect to \mathbf{u}.

Proof. Standard; see Hale & Lunel (1993) or Pepe & Jiang (2006). ∎

We will use the specific functional

V(\mathbf{x}_t) = \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{x}(t)^\top \mathbf{P}\mathbf{x}(t) + \int_{-\tau}^0 \mathbf{x}(t+\theta)^\top \mathbf{Q} \mathbf{x}(t+\theta)\,d\theta,
\]  

and verify the derivative condition in Chapter 5 after modelling the neuromechanical and aerodynamic delays.

2.3 Control Barrier Functions and Forward Invariance

Consider a control‑affine system

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}) + \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{u}, \quad \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{D} \subset \mathbb{R}^n,
\]  

with \mathbf{f}, \mathbf{g} locally Lipschitz.

Definition 2.4 (Control Barrier Function, CBF). Let h : \mathcal{D} \to \mathbb{R} be continuously differentiable and define the safe set \mathcal{C} = \{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{D} : h(\mathbf{x}) \ge 0\}. The function h is a control barrier function for \mathcal{C} if there exists an extended class \mathcal{K} function \alpha such that

\sup_{\mathbf{u}} \big[ L_{\mathbf{f}}h(\mathbf{x}) + L_{\mathbf{g}}h(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{u} + \alpha(h(\mathbf{x})) \big] \ge 0, \quad \forall \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{D}.

Theorem 2.2 (Forward Invariance). If h is a CBF on \mathcal{D} and \mathbf{u}(\mathbf{x}) is a Lipschitz continuous controller satisfying the CBF inequality for all \mathbf{x}, then the set \mathcal{C} is forward‑invariant: \mathbf{x}(0) \in \mathcal{C} \implies \mathbf{x}(t) \in \mathcal{C} for all t \ge 0.

Proof. Nagumo’s theorem applied to the differential inclusion. ∎

Robustness to bounded uncertainty. If the true dynamics are \dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}) + \Delta\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}) + \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{u} with \|\Delta\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x})\| \le \epsilon(\mathbf{x}), then the tightened CBF condition

L_{\mathbf{f}}h + L_{\mathbf{g}}h\,\mathbf{u} + \alpha(h) - \|\nabla h\| \epsilon(\mathbf{x}) \ge 0
\]  

guarantees invariance. This will be used to account for harness flex and soft‑tissue compliance.

2.4 Rate‑Distortion Theory for Vector Gaussian Sources

Definition 2.5 (Mutual Information). For two random vectors \mathbf{X}, \hat{\mathbf{X}} with joint density p(\mathbf{x},\hat{\mathbf{x}}), the mutual information is

I(\mathbf{X};\hat{\mathbf{X}}) = \iint p(\mathbf{x},\hat{\mathbf{x}}) \log \frac{p(\mathbf{x},\hat{\mathbf{x}})}{p(\mathbf{x})p(\hat{\mathbf{x}})} \,d\mathbf{x}\,d\hat{\mathbf{x}}.

Definition 2.6 (Rate‑Distortion Function). For a source \mathbf{X} \in \mathbb{R}^m and a distortion measure d(\mathbf{x},\hat{\mathbf{x}}) \ge 0, the rate‑distortion function R(D) is the infimum of mutual informations I(\mathbf{X};\hat{\mathbf{X}}) over all conditional distributions p(\hat{\mathbf{x}}|\mathbf{x}) satisfying \mathbb{E}[d(\mathbf{X},\hat{\mathbf{X}})] \le D.

Theorem 2.3 (Vector Gaussian Rate‑Distortion). Let \mathbf{X} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}) with \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \succ 0, and let the distortion be weighted squared error d(\mathbf{x},\hat{\mathbf{x}}) = (\mathbf{x} - \hat{\mathbf{x}})^\top \mathbf{W} (\mathbf{x} - \hat{\mathbf{x}}) with \mathbf{W} \succ 0. Then

R(D) = \min_{\mathbf{D} \succeq \mathbf{0},\; \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{W}\mathbf{D}) \le D} \frac{1}{2} \log \frac{\det \boldsymbol{\Sigma}}{\det \mathbf{D}},
\]  

where the minimum is achieved by a reverse water‑filling solution over the eigenvalues of \mathbf{W}^{1/2}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\mathbf{W}^{1/2}. In particular,

R(D) = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^m \log \frac{\lambda_i}{\theta_i},
\]  

with \theta_i = \min(\lambda_i, \nu), and \nu chosen so that \sum \theta_i = D.

Proof. See Cover & Thomas (2006), Chapter 10. ∎

This theorem will be applied to the motor‑intent vector \mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^8 (for a single subsystem) or \mathbb{R}^{16} (combined legs and wings), with a distortion weight \mathbf{W} that penalises errors in terms of their impact on gait stability and stall margin.

2.5 The Spring‑Loaded Inverted Pendulum (SLIP) and Gait Stability

The SLIP model is the canonical template for high‑speed running (Blickhan, 1989). The body is a point mass m supported by a massless linear spring of stiffness k and rest length L_0. During stance, with foot fixed at origin, the dynamics are

m\ddot{x} = -k(L_0 - L)\frac{x}{L}, \quad m\ddot{z} = -k(L_0 - L)\frac{z}{L} - mg,
\]  

with L = \sqrt{x^2 + z^2}. Flight phase is ballistic. The apex return map P: (v_x, z_{\text{apex}}) \mapsto (v_x', z_{\text{apex}}') maps state from one apex to the next.

Definition 2.7 (Orbital Stability). A periodic running orbit is orbitally exponentially stable if the linearised Poincaré map \mathbf{J}_P has all eigenvalues strictly inside the unit circle.

Proposition 2.4 (Touchdown Angle Sensitivity). For a nominal stable orbit with forward speed v^* and touchdown angle \theta^*, the maximum allowable deviation \Delta\theta_{\text{crit}} such that the orbit remains within the basin of attraction is obtained by numerically computing the parameter bifurcation at which the dominant Floquet multiplier crosses unity. A closed‑form approximation for high stiffness is

\Delta\theta_{\text{crit}} \approx \arctan\!\left( \frac{2\sqrt{mgL_0/k}}{v^*} \right),
\]  

which for m = 70\,\mathrm{kg}, k = 30\,\mathrm{kN/m}, L_0 = 1.0\,\mathrm{m}, v^* = 15\,\mathrm{m/s} yields ≈ 8.2°.

Derivation. Linearising the stance dynamics around the nominal trajectory and solving for the transition matrix at lift‑off yields this approximation; the full numeric computation is provided in Appendix A. We will use this margin as the safe‑distortion limit for the leg subsystem.

2.6 Unsteady Aerodynamics and Dynamic Stall Margin

Flapping wings at human scale operate at reduced frequencies k = \frac{\omega c}{2U} \sim 1, where quasi‑steady aerodynamics fail. We adopt a semi‑empirical model based on the Leishman–Beddoes dynamic stall formulation, simplified to a single lag in effective angle of attack:

\tau_{\text{stall}} \dot{\alpha}_{\text{eff}} + \alpha_{\text{eff}} = \alpha(t),
\]  

with \tau_{\text{stall}} \approx 0.1\,\mathrm{s} for a wing of chord c = 0.5\,\mathrm{m} at U = 10\,\mathrm{m/s}. The lift coefficient is

C_L(\alpha_{\text{eff}}, \dot{\alpha}) = C_{L,\text{static}}(\alpha_{\text{eff}}) + \Delta C_{L,\text{dyn}}(\dot{\alpha}).
\]  

Cycle‑averaged lift is computed via blade‑element integration (Appendix B). The critical stall margin \Delta\alpha_{\text{stall}} is the deviation from the optimum feathering angle beyond which the cycle‑averaged lift drops below weight. For the prototype parameters (R = 1.5\,\mathrm{m}, S = 4\,\mathrm{m}^2, \omega = 3\,\mathrm{Hz}) we find \Delta\alpha_{\text{stall}} \approx 12^\circ.

