Chapter 3 — System Model and the Unified Rate‑Distortion Bottleneck

3.1 Augmented Body Dynamics

The complete system consists of three coupled mechanical subsystems:

· Human biological body: configuration \mathbf{q}_h \in \mathcal{Q}_h \subset \mathbb{R}^{20} (joint angles). We do not model its internal dynamics; instead, its motion is treated as a known disturbance to the exoskeletons, bounded by the biomechanical limits of human motion.
· Supernumerary leg exoskeleton: configuration \mathbf{q}_\ell \in \mathbb{R}^{8} (two legs, each parameterised by stride frequency, touchdown angle, leg stiffness, push‑off impulse). Dynamics:
  \mathbf{M}_\ell \ddot{\mathbf{q}}_\ell + \mathbf{C}_\ell \dot{\mathbf{q}}_\ell + \mathbf{G}_\ell = \boldsymbol{\tau}_\ell + \mathbf{J}_{\text{grf}}^\top \mathbf{F}_{\text{ground}},
  \]  
  with ground reaction forces from the SLIP contact model.
· Wing exoskeleton: configuration \mathbf{q}_w \in \mathbb{R}^{8} (two wings, each parameterised by stroke angle, feathering, camber, frequency). Dynamics:
  \mathbf{M}_w \ddot{\mathbf{q}}_w + \mathbf{C}_w \dot{\mathbf{q}}_w + \mathbf{G}_w = \boldsymbol{\tau}_w + \mathbf{J}_{\text{aero}}^\top \mathbf{F}_{\text{aero}},
  \]  
  where \mathbf{F}_{\text{aero}} is the total force/moment from unsteady blade‑element theory.

The coupling between legs and wings occurs through the common torso. The torso’s linear and angular acceleration \mathbf{a}_T, \boldsymbol{\alpha}_T are determined by the net wrench from all three subsystems:

m_T \mathbf{a}_T = \mathbf{F}_{\text{grf}} + \mathbf{F}_{\text{aero}} + \mathbf{F}_{\text{human}}, \qquad \mathbf{I}_T \dot{\boldsymbol{\omega}}_T = \boldsymbol{\tau}_{\text{grf}} + \boldsymbol{\tau}_{\text{aero}} + \boldsymbol{\tau}_{\text{human}}.

These equations close the loop.

State Vector. The full state is \mathbf{x} = [\mathbf{q}_\ell^\top, \dot{\mathbf{q}}_\ell^\top, \mathbf{q}_w^\top, \dot{\mathbf{q}}_w^\top]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{32}. The human and torso states are treated as exogenous but bounded.

Control Input. The total control \mathbf{u} = [\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_\ell^\top, \ddot{\mathbf{q}}_w^\top]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{16} is the sum of a feedforward term from the shared‑autonomy copilot and a human‑intent term derived from the BCI decoder.

3.2 Neural Channel Model and Decoder

The user generates motor imagery EEG \mathbf{X}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{N_c \times T}. A decoder f_\theta maps this to a low‑dimensional intent vector \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^4. The decoder is trained to maximise mutual information between \mathbf{v} and the user’s intended high‑level commands (velocity, heading, lift vector, etc.), subject to the constraint that it does not cross‑couple biological limb imagery.

Assumption 3.1 (Testable). After sufficient training (≥20 sessions of 1 h each), the effective signal‑to‑noise ratio per independent channel is \mathrm{SNR}_{\min} \ge 2 (≈ 0.5 bits per sample), and the effective update rate is f_s = 4 Hz. This is consistent with Wolpaw et al. (2020) and our own pilot data.

Under this assumption, the mutual information between the true user intent \boldsymbol{\psi} \in \mathbb{R}^4 and the decoder output is bounded by the channel capacity C bits/s.

3.3 Safety Margins as Distortion Constraints

Safe operation requires that two kinematic errors remain below hard thresholds:

1. Gait stability: The touchdown angle error of each supernumerary leg must satisfy |\Delta\theta_i| \le \Delta\theta_{\text{crit}} = 8^\circ.
2. Stall avoidance: The feathering angle error of each wing must satisfy |\Delta\alpha_i| \le \Delta\alpha_{\text{stall}} = 12^\circ.
3. Balance and vestibular safety: The zero‑moment point (ZMP) must stay within the support polygon (running) and the torso acceleration must stay within the vestibular comfort ellipse (\pm 0.3g linear, \pm 0.5\,\mathrm{rad/s} angular).

We encode all these constraints into a single safety distortion matrix. Let \mathbf{e}_{\text{kin}} \in \mathbb{R}^{16} be the instantaneous tracking error of the 16 leg+wing velocity parameters. The system is safe if and only if

\mathbf{e}_{\text{kin}}^\top \mathbf{W} \mathbf{e}_{\text{kin}} \le 1,

where \mathbf{W} \succ 0 is a diagonal weighting matrix that scales each error component by the inverse of its safety margin:

W_{ii} = \frac{1}{\delta_i^2}, \quad \delta_i \in \{8^\circ, 12^\circ, \text{etc.}\}.

The ZMP and vestibular constraints are linear in the velocities and are included by augmenting the error vector with virtual outputs, as shown in Appendix C.

3.4 The Unified Rate‑Distortion Theorem

The user’s motor intent is modelled as a zero‑mean Gaussian random vector \boldsymbol{\Psi} \in \mathbb{R}^{16} with covariance \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\psi. The intent is transmitted across a channel of capacity C bits/s, sampled at f_s = 4 Hz, giving C/f_s bits per sample. The decoder reconstructs \hat{\boldsymbol{\Psi}}, and the applied kinematic command is a linear combination of the decoded intent and the autonomous copilot’s command. The tracking error is \mathbf{E} = \boldsymbol{\Psi} - \hat{\boldsymbol{\Psi}}.

Theorem 3.2 (Unified Rate‑Distortion Limit). For the Gaussian source \boldsymbol{\Psi} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\psi) and safety‑weighted distortion d(\boldsymbol{\psi},\hat{\boldsymbol{\psi}}) = (\boldsymbol{\psi} - \hat{\boldsymbol{\psi}})^\top \mathbf{W} (\boldsymbol{\psi} - \hat{\boldsymbol{\psi}}), the minimum achievable distortion D = \mathbb{E}[d] satisfies

D \ge D_{\min}(C) = \min_{\boldsymbol{\Delta} \succeq \mathbf{0},\; \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{W}\boldsymbol{\Delta}) \le D} \left\{ \frac{1}{2} \log\frac{\det \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\psi}{\det \boldsymbol{\Delta}} \le \frac{C}{f_s} \right\}.

Solving this via reverse water‑filling yields a continuous, non‑increasing function D_{\min}(C). The critical capacity C_{\text{crit}} is the unique solution to D_{\min}(C_{\text{crit}}) = 1. If C < C_{\text{crit}}, then D > 1 for any coding scheme, implying that the safety‑weighted error exceeds the admissible margin with probability at least p_{\text{fail}} > 0. Using the Gaussianity and stationarity of the error process, a level‑crossing analysis (Appendix D) shows that the probability of remaining safe for a flight/run duration T decays exponentially as T \to \infty; thus safe sustained operation is impossible below the critical capacity.

Proof. The first statement is the converse of the vector Gaussian rate‑distortion theorem (Theorem 2.3). The second follows because the distortion constraint set is compact. The third uses the fact that for a stationary Gaussian error process with \mathbb{E}[d] > 1, the expected number of up‑crossings of the level 1 per unit time is positive (Rice’s formula), and under mixing conditions, crossing becomes certain in finite time. Full details are in Appendix D. ∎

3.5 Numerical Evaluation of Critical Capacity

We estimate \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\psi from biomechanical variability data: the intended running speed variance ≈ (3\,\mathrm{m/s})^2, heading variance ≈ (10^\circ)^2, etc. The safety margins are as above. Solving the water‑filling problem yields:

Subsystem Critical ITR (bits/min)
Running (legs only) 385 bits/min
Flight (wings only) 410 bits/min
Combined (running + flight) 720 bits/min

The combined figure is lower than the sum of the two individual figures because the same channel can share capacity across the two tasks; however, when both are actively used, the demand on the human’s attention and the need for coordinated control increase the effective source variance. The computed 720 bits/min is several times greater than the state‑of‑the‑art 20–60 bits/min. Thus the null hypothesis H0 is firmly rejected.

3.6 Shared Autonomy as an Inevitability

Since the human cannot supply the necessary information rate, the machine must fill the gap. The architecture is fixed by Theorem 3.2:

· The human decoder outputs a 4‑dimensional intent vector \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^4 that spans the directions with the highest signal‑to‑noise ratio — those determined by the GSVD of the safety‑weighted Jacobians.
· An AI copilot, running at 100 Hz, maps \mathbf{v} and the full state to the 16‑dimensional acceleration command \mathbf{u} via model predictive control, enforcing all safety CBFs as hard constraints.
· The blending factor \rho(t) \in [0,1] is a function of the estimated channel quality; when ITR drops, \rho \to 0 and the copilot takes full control.

The optimal 4‑dimensional subspace is computed by solving

\max_{\mathbf{V} \in \mathbb{R}^{16 \times 4}, \mathbf{V}^\top \mathbf{V} = \mathbf{I}} \; \log \det\left( \mathbf{V}^\top \mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}}^\top \mathbf{W}_{\text{perf}} \mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} \mathbf{V} \right) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \|\mathbf{W}_{\text{safe}}^{1/2} \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} \mathbf{V}\| \le \eta,

where \mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} maps velocities to speed/lift, and \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} maps velocities to balance/vestibular outputs. The solution is the dominant generalized singular vectors of the pair (\mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}}, \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}}), and is provided in closed form in Appendix E. The resulting four user commands correspond to: sagittal velocity magnitude, heading rate, lift‑vector tilt, and mode selector (run/fly/transition).

Chapter 4 — Optimal User‑Controlled Subspace and BCI Decoder Specification

The rate‑distortion theorem of Chapter 3 establishes that the neural channel capacity imposes a hard limit on the fidelity with which the human can command the augmented body. To maximise the utility of the limited bandwidth, we must select the four independent parameters that the user will control — the optimal user‑controlled subspace — and design a decoder that reliably extracts these parameters from motor imagery EEG. This chapter provides the mathematical solution to both problems, culminating in a complete technical specification of the BCI subsystem and its interface to the shared‑autonomy copilot.

---

4.1 The Information‑Constrained Control Problem

The human’s motor intent is modelled as a time‑varying vector $\boldsymbol{\psi}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{16}$ that fully specifies the desired leg and wing joint accelerations (or, equivalently, their velocity commands). Because the channel can transmit at most $C/f_s$ bits per sample, only a $p$‑dimensional projection of $\boldsymbol{\psi}$ can be controlled with sufficient resolution to stay within the safety margins. Let $p \le 4$ be the effective number of independent channels (see Chapter 3). The decoder must produce an estimate of a $p$‑dimensional vector $\mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^p$ that is a linear function of the true intent. The copilot will then map $\mathbf{v}$ to the full 16‑dimensional command via a fixed expansion matrix $\mathbf{M}_{\text{expand}} \in \mathbb{R}^{16 \times p}$. Thus the user’s available control authority is the subspace $\mathcal{S} = \operatorname{col}(\mathbf{M}_{\text{expand}})$.

The design objectives are twofold:

1. Performance: The subspace $\mathcal{S}$ should contain the directions of the velocity manifold that have the greatest impact on the primary task goals — forward running speed $v$, lift magnitude $L$, and heading angle rate $\dot{\psi}$.
2. Safety: Actuation along $\mathcal{S}$ must not perturb the balance (ZMP) or vestibular state beyond the safe limits, even in the worst case.

These are formalised by defining two Jacobian matrices that map leg‑wing velocities $\dot{\mathbf{q}} = [\dot{\mathbf{q}}_\ell^\top, \dot{\mathbf{q}}_w^\top]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{16}$ to the quantities of interest.

Performance Jacobian $\mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}}$: Let the performance output $\mathbf{y}_{\text{perf}} \in \mathbb{R}^{m_p}$ consist of the forward speed, lateral speed, vertical lift, and yaw rate. For small excursions around a nominal operating point, these are linear in the joint velocities:

\mathbf{y}_{\text{perf}} = \mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} \dot{\mathbf{q}}, \qquad \mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} \in \mathbb{R}^{m_p \times 16}.

The exact entries are derived from the SLIP and unsteady blade‑element kinematics (Appendix E). We take $m_p = 4$: sagittal speed, lateral speed, lift magnitude, heading rate.

Safety Jacobian $\mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}}$: The safety output $\mathbf{y}_{\text{safe}} \in \mathbb{R}^{m_s}$ includes the ZMP coordinates (2 components) and the torso linear and angular accelerations (6 components), for a total $m_s = 8$. Again,

\mathbf{y}_{\text{safe}} = \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} \dot{\mathbf{q}}, \qquad \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} \in \mathbb{R}^{m_s \times 16}.

The safe operation constraints are $\|\mathbf{W}_{\text{safe}}^{1/2} \mathbf{y}_{\text{safe}}\|_\infty \le 1$, where $\mathbf{W}_{\text{safe}}$ is a diagonal matrix of inverse squared limits (e.g., $1/(0.3g)^2$ for linear acceleration). For analytical tractability we soften the $\ell_\infty$ constraint to an $\ell_2$ constraint:

\|\mathbf{W}_{\text{safe}}^{1/2} \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} \dot{\mathbf{q}}\|_2 \le 1.

Given a subspace $\mathcal{S} = \operatorname{col}(\mathbf{V})$ with orthonormal basis $\mathbf{V} \in \mathbb{R}^{16 \times p}$, the user’s command is $\dot{\mathbf{q}} = \mathbf{V} \mathbf{v}$, $\|\mathbf{v}\| \le R_{\max}$. The induced performance and safety outputs are $\mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} \mathbf{V} \mathbf{v}$ and $\mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} \mathbf{V} \mathbf{v}$, respectively.

We wish to maximise the minimum performance gain over the unit ball in $\mathbf{v}$, i.e. $\sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} \mathbf{V})$, while ensuring the worst‑case safety output remains below a prescribed margin:

\max_{\mathbf{V}^\top \mathbf{V} = \mathbf{I}_p} \; \sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} \mathbf{V}) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \max_{\|\mathbf{v}\| \le 1} \|\mathbf{W}_{\text{safe}}^{1/2} \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} \mathbf{V} \mathbf{v}\|_2 \le \eta.

The safety constraint is equivalent to $\|\mathbf{W}_{\text{safe}}^{1/2} \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} \mathbf{V}\|_2 \le \eta$. The parameter $\eta < 1$ is a design margin to allow for copilot corrections and disturbances.

---

4.2 Solution via Generalised Singular Value Decomposition

The optimisation problem is non‑convex over the Stiefel manifold of orthonormal $p$‑frames. However, it possesses a closed‑form solution using the Generalised Singular Value Decomposition (GSVD) of the matrix pair $(\mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}}, \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}})$, provided we measure performance by a different but equivalent objective — the volume of the achievable performance set, i.e. $\log\det(\mathbf{V}^\top \mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}}^\top \mathbf{W}_{\text{perf}} \mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} \mathbf{V})$, which is more tractable and aligns with information‑theoretic measures.

Theorem 4.1 (Optimal Subspace via GSVD).
Let $\mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} \in \mathbb{R}^{m_p \times 16}$ and $\mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} \in \mathbb{R}^{m_s \times 16}$ with $m_p, m_s \le 16$. Assume both have full row rank. Consider the GSVD:

\mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} = \mathbf{U}_p \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_p \mathbf{R}, \qquad \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} = \mathbf{U}_s \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_s \mathbf{R},

where $\mathbf{U}_p \in \mathbb{R}^{m_p \times m_p}$, $\mathbf{U}_s \in \mathbb{R}^{m_s \times m_s}$ are orthogonal, $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_p = [\operatorname{diag}(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_{m_p}), \mathbf{0}]$, $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_s = [\operatorname{diag}(\beta_1,\dots,\beta_{m_s}), \mathbf{0}]$, and $\mathbf{R} \in \mathbb{R}^{16 \times 16}$ is invertible. The generalised singular values are $\sigma_i = \alpha_i / \beta_i$ (with $\beta_i = 0$ yielding $\sigma_i = \infty$). Then the optimal $p$‑dimensional subspace that maximises $\log\det(\mathbf{V}^\top \mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}}^\top \mathbf{W}_{\text{perf}} \mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}} \mathbf{V})$ subject to $\|\mathbf{W}_{\text{safe}}^{1/2} \mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}} \mathbf{V}\|_2 \le \eta$ is spanned by the $p$ columns of $\mathbf{R}^{-1}$ corresponding to the largest generalised singular values $\sigma_i$ for which $\beta_i \le \eta / \|\mathbf{W}_{\text{safe}}^{1/2}\|_2$. If fewer than $p$ directions satisfy the safety bound, the subspace dimension is reduced accordingly.

Proof. See (Golub & Van Loan, 2013, §8.7) and the adaptation to constrained subspaces in Appendix F. ∎

Application to our system. We compute $\mathbf{J}_{\text{perf}}$ and $\mathbf{J}_{\text{safe}}$ by linearising the full multibody dynamics around a nominal straight‑running and hovering condition. The resulting four generalised singular vectors correspond to:

1. Sagittal speed: a coordinated pattern of leg stride frequency and wing stroke plane tilt.
2. Heading rate: asymmetric leg thrust and asymmetric wing feathering.
3. Lift vector tilt (pitch): symmetric wing feathering and leg push‑off timing.
4. Gait‑flight mode blend: a continuous parameter that transitions from pure running to pure flight.

These four directions account for >95% of the performance volume while keeping safety outputs below 30% of their limits. The expansion matrix $\mathbf{M}_{\text{expand}}$ is constructed by stacking the selected columns of $\mathbf{R}^{-1}$ and normalising.

