6.3 Fallback and Transition Protocols

The copilot includes a finite‑state machine that reacts to critical events, ensuring graceful degradation. The states and transitions are:

State Description Trigger Action
NORMAL Shared autonomy active ITR ≥ 50 bits/min & no faults Normal MPC
CAUTION Reduced human authority 20 ≤ ITR < 50 or mild actuator overheat $\rho$ capped at 0.5, conservative speed limits
AUTO Full copilot control ITR < 20 bits/min or BCI signal loss $\rho = 0$, copilot holds current speed/altitude or transitions to safe jog/hover. Auditory alert: “autonomy engaged.”
FAULT_LEG Single leg actuator failure Torque sensor mismatch > threshold Asymmetric gait recovery: contralateral leg stiffness increased, speed reduced by 40%. Transition to stable limp in < 3 strides.
FAULT_WING Single wing actuator failure Torque sensor mismatch Emergency asymmetric descent: healthy wing maintains partial lift, copilot executes controlled spiral descent to ground.
POWER_LOW Battery < 10% Voltage monitor $\rho = 0$, all unnecessary DOFs locked; wings feather to minimise drag; legs switch to passive spring mode for energy‑efficient running.
INCAPACITATION Gyro detects fall / loss of postural control Orientation exceeds 60° from vertical Immediate full autonomy; wings deploy airbrakes and legs execute a pre‑programmed crouch. If airborne, autorotation‑like descent using wing drag. GPS emergency beacon activated.

All transitions are accompanied by haptic and auditory cues to keep the user informed.

