Chapter 7 — Experimental Validation Roadmap

A theory, no matter how mathematically rigorous, remains a speculation until it is tested against physical reality. This chapter describes a three‑phase experimental programme designed to falsify the central informational‑ceiling hypothesis with progressively more realistic hardware. Each phase is self‑contained and provides a binary criterion for rejecting H1; should the data contradict the rate‑distortion bound, the entire framework collapses.

---

7.1 Phase 1 – Minimum Viable Experiment (Year 1)

Objective. Test whether the trade‑off between BCI information transfer rate (ITR) and kinematic tracking error follows the predicted vector rate‑distortion curve, and whether below the critical capacity loss of stability becomes inevitable in a virtual environment.

Setup.

· Participants: 12 healthy adults, no prior BCI experience.
· EEG: 64‑channel active electrodes (g.USBamp, 512 Hz).
· VR environment: A high‑fidelity Unity simulation of the full leg‑wing avatar, running on an immersive treadmill (Motek CAREN) and a 270° projection screen. The virtual legs and wings are rendered as transparent spring‑masses; the first‑person viewpoint includes an artificial horizon, speed tape, and a ZMP/vestibular safety ring.
· Task: The user attempts to follow a moving target (a bird) along a winding track that alternates between running and flight segments. The target’s speed and lift requirements vary sinusoidally. The user controls the four‑dimensional intent vector via two‑class motor imagery initially mapped to speed up/down (left‑hand vs. right‑hand) and lift up/down (foot vs. tongue), with continuous regression trained as described in Chapter 4. The copilot handles the remaining 12 DOFs.
· Independent variable: BCI ITR, measured online via the Wolpaw proxy (bits/min). Training sessions (20 sessions over 5 weeks) naturally increase ITR from ≈10 to ≈60 bits/min.
· Dependent variables:
  1. Tracking error RMS: Euclidean distance between the target velocity/lift vector and the avatar’s actual velocity/lift, normalised by safety margins.
  2. Virtual stability events: Number of “gait bifurcation” events (leg touchdown angle deviation >8° for >2 consecutive strides) and “dynamic stall” events (wing feathering angle deviation >12° for >2 strokes) per minute.
  3. Subjective embodiment: Rubber‑hand/body‑transfer illusion questionnaire.

Prediction and falsification criterion.
The rate‑distortion theorem (Theorem 3.2) predicts that the safety‑weighted distortion $D = \mathbb{E}[ \mathbf{e}^\top \mathbf{W} \mathbf{e}]$ satisfies $D \ge \sigma_\psi^2 2^{-2C/f_s}$. For the prototype parameters, the critical capacity for the combined task is $C_{\text{crit}} = 720$ bits/min. Since participants will never exceed 60 bits/min, every participant should experience a virtual stability event rate that grows exponentially with decreasing ITR. Specifically, below 60 bits/min, the event rate should exceed 2/min (a threshold for “unstable” flight/run).
Falsification: If more than 20% of participants achieve ITR < 60 bits/min and yet maintain a stability event rate <0.5/min over a 10‑minute continuous trial, the null hypothesis cannot be rejected and H1 is falsified.

Statistical analysis.
Mixed‑effects Poisson regression of event rate on ITR, with random intercepts for participants. The expected coefficient is strongly negative.

---

7.2 Phase 2 – Physical Prototype Wind‑Tunnel and Treadmill Validation (Year 2)

Objective. Validate the unsteady aerodynamic and SLIP models, identify the harness compliance bound $\epsilon$, and demonstrate closed‑loop shared‑autonomy running with a single leg exoskeleton and stationary flight with a single wing in a wind tunnel.

Hardware specifications.

· Wing prototype: 1:2 scale of the full wing, carbon‑fibre frame, 3‑D printed nylon skin, actuated by three brushless DC motors (stroke, feathering, camber). Instrumented with 6‑axis load cell at root. Tested in a low‑speed wind tunnel at 10 m/s.
· Leg prototype: A single supernumerary leg with series‑elastic actuator (peak force 3 kN), instrumented treadmill with force plates.
· Copilot hardware: NVIDIA Jetson Orin, running the MPC at 100 Hz; safety harness with measured compliance.

Measurements.

· Dynamic stall parameters: With sinusoidal flapping (frequency sweeps 1–5 Hz, amplitude ±30°), measure unsteady lift and drag. Fit the first‑order lag model $\tau_{\text{stall}}$ and the lift enhancement factor $\Gamma$ via least‑squares. Confirm the stall margin $\Delta\alpha_{\text{stall}} = 12^\circ$.
· SLIP bifurcation: Command sinusoidal variations in touchdown angle while running at 10 m/s on the treadmill. Measure the amplitude at which the stride‑to‑stride return map loses its fixed point; this yields the empirical $\Delta\theta_{\text{crit}}$. Compare with the derived 8°.
· Harness compliance: Apply known wrenches to the exoskeleton frame and measure displacement at the human interface; the worst‑case norm $\epsilon$ of the Jacobian uncertainty is computed.

Failure criterion. If any key parameter ($\tau_{\text{stall}}$, $\Delta\theta_{\text{crit}}$, $\epsilon$) deviates from the modelled value by more than 50%, the theoretical margins must be recomputed, and the entire safety analysis revisited.

