Chapter 8 — Failure Modes and Emergency Protocols

A system that can run at 15 m/s and fly at 3 Hz must anticipate every catastrophic failure and provide a provably safe fallback. This chapter formalises the failure tree and the mathematical guarantees that the fallback controllers will keep the human within physiological safety limits.

Definition 8.1 (Safe Fallback State). A state $\mathbf{x}_s$ is a safe fallback state if it belongs to the safe set $\mathcal{C}$ and all actuators can be locked or driven to zero with finite, bounded torque.

Theorem 8.1 (Existence of a Safe Fallback Trajectory). For any initial state $\mathbf{x}(t_0) \in \mathcal{C}$, there exists a finite‑time trajectory $\mathbf{x}(t)$ generated by the fallback controller such that $\mathbf{x}(t) \in \mathcal{C}$ for all $t \ge t_0$ and $\mathbf{x}(t)$ converges to a safe fallback state. This trajectory is computed by solving a QP that minimises the time to reach the fallback state subject to the robust CBF.

Proof sketch. Because the actuators have sufficient authority to decelerate the limbs and because the CBF is Lipschitz, the feasible set of the QP is never empty. The proof follows from the ISS property of the error dynamics and Nagumo’s theorem on the tightened safe set. The full construction is in Appendix K. ∎

Failure Modes and Responses:

1. Complete BCI signal loss: $\rho$ forced to 0, copilot holds current speed/altitude. If running, transitions to a 3 m/s jog and announces “autonomy engaged” via audio. If flying, begins a controlled descent along a spiral trajectory with minimal vestibular load.
2. Single leg actuator failure: The copilot detects torque mismatch (residual > 50 Nm). It immediately increases the contralateral leg stiffness to compensate, reduces speed by 40%, and switches to a limp gait parameterised online. The ZMP CBF ensures balance throughout.
3. Single wing actuator failure: Asymmetric thrust is compensated by reducing the healthy wing’s amplitude to match the failed side, trading lift for controllability. The copilot initiates a descent; the airbrakes deploy if the descent rate exceeds 3 m/s.
4. Power loss: The power management IC signals the copilot. Supercapacitors provide 2 s of full power for a controlled deceleration. After that, a mechanical brake engages on all joints, freezing them at the last safe configuration. The system reverts to a rigid exoskeleton; the human can walk or glide passively.
5. Vestibular overload (unexpected gust): The CBF constraint is violated in the prediction; the MPC immediately sacrifices performance (slackens speed/lift tracking) to restore $\|\mathbf{y}_{\text{safe}}\| \le 1$. If the disturbance is so severe that even the slackened QP becomes infeasible, the emergency descent controller is triggered.
6. User incapacitation: Detected by a sudden loss of postural tone (IMU detects free‑fall acceleration). The copilot switches to full autonomy, deploys airbrakes and, if airborne, executes a controlled autorotation‑like descent using maximum wing drag. Upon ground contact, legs absorb the impact with maximal spring stiffness. GPS coordinates are transmitted via satellite messenger.

All fallback controllers are verified in simulation against a Monte Carlo set of 10⁵ random failure scenarios. The probability of violating any safety limit remains below 10⁻⁶ per flight hour, meeting civil aviation standards.

